{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791123973,"nonce":"GfQKNLfvPPgdgGm42SNhPYM3fQQ=","message":"Implemented as route 3, the Blinc post-layout pass, with no taffy fork.\n\nLayoutTree::align_baselines runs after taffy, walks the tree deepest\nfirst, and for each baseline-aligned flex line shifts every item onto\nthe deepest baseline. The shifts live in a side table and are added in\nget_bounds and get_absolute_bounds. taffy's own stored layout is left\nalone, because there is no public way to write it.\n\nFindings worth recording, several of which only showed up on screen:\n\n1. The shift must be measured in the PARENT. My first version aligned\n   each item's within-box offset and ignored that taffy had already\n   placed the boxes at different tops, which left them apart by exactly\n   that difference. A test caught it: 26px of divergence where there\n   should have been none.\n\n2. The paint path reads LayoutTree::get_bounds, not\n   get_absolute_bounds. Applying the shift only to the latter made every\n   test pass while the rendered frame was byte-identical. Only a\n   screenshot diff showed it.\n\n3. A non-wrapping text leaf has no measure context. Text::build gives\n   fixed dimensions and no TextMeasureContext when wrap is false, so\n   there was nothing to measure a baseline from, and that is the common\n   inline case. The builder now reports the baseline it already\n   measured, via LayoutTree::set_text_baseline.\n\n4. The half-leading change is far narrower than feared. TextVerticalAlign\n   defaults to Top, and the Top branch already centres glyphs in the line\n   box, which puts the first baseline at half-leading plus ascender. Only\n   the Baseline branch drew at top + ascender, so the two anchors\n   disagreed. Moving it makes Baseline agree with Top rather than\n   shifting all text: cn_demo renders pixel-identical before and after.\n\n5. Centre-anchored text opts out. It is optically centred by cap height,\n   so it has no first baseline to align by and keeps taffy's placement.\n\n6. Seven copies of `baseline_y = text.y + text.ascender` had to move\n   together, the same pattern as the text-inheritance copies.\n\nVerified on screen as well as in tests: 16px sans with a 14px mono chip,\nand 32/12/20px sans, all share a baseline where before they diverged by\nup to 8px.","files":null}]}